// ALG1 Unit 04 Lesson 04 Quiz
// Lesson: Domain and Range
// MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support - no new primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q01] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
What is the domain of the relation \((2,5)\), \((4,9)\), \((6,13)\)?
{
={2, 4, 6}#Correct. The domain is the set of input values, or x-values.
~{5, 9, 13}#These are output values, so they form the range.
~{2, 5, 4, 9, 6, 13}#This combines inputs and outputs instead of isolating the domain.
~{2, 6, 5, 13}#This mixes inputs with outputs and leaves out values.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q02] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
What is the range of the relation \((2,5)\), \((4,9)\), \((6,13)\)?
{
~{2, 4, 6}#These are input values, so they form the domain.
~{2, 5, 4, 9, 6, 13}#This combines every value. The range includes only outputs.
={5, 9, 13}#Correct. The range is the set of output values, or y-values.
~{4, 9, 13}#This includes one input and omits one output.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q03] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A table has inputs \(-1, 0, 1, 2\) and outputs \(7, 4, 1, -2\). What is the domain?
{
~{7, 4, 1, -2}#These are outputs, so they form the range.
= {-1, 0, 1, 2}#Correct. The domain is the set of inputs.
~{-2, 1, 4, 7}#This is the range written in increasing order, not the domain.
~{-1, 7, 0, 4, 1, 2, -2}#This mixes inputs and outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q04] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A graph has points \((0,3)\), \((1,5)\), and \((2,7)\). What is the range?
{
~{0, 1, 2}#These are the x-values, so they form the domain.
~{0, 3, 1, 5, 2, 7}#This lists both inputs and outputs.
~{1, 2, 3}#This is not the set of y-values shown by the points.
={3, 5, 7}#Correct. The range is the set of y-values.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q05] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
Which set is a reasonable domain for the number of students in a classroom?
{
~All real numbers#A student count cannot include fractions or negative values.
=Whole numbers such as 0, 1, 2, 3, ...#Correct. A count of students must be a nonnegative whole number.
~All negative integers#A classroom cannot have a negative number of students.
~Only decimals between 0 and 1#Student counts are whole-number counts, not decimals.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q06::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q06] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A runner tracks distance from 0 to 5 miles during a workout. Which range is reasonable for the distance run?
{
=0 to 5 miles, inclusive#Correct. The distance can start at 0, end at 5, and include values between.
~Only 5 miles#The runner has distances less than 5 before finishing.
~All real numbers#Distances in this context cannot be negative or exceed the workout distance.
~5 miles or more#The workout is capped at 5 miles.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q07::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q07] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
For the ordered pairs \((-3,8)\), \((-1,4)\), and \((1,0)\), which set is the domain?
{
~{8, 4, 0}#These are output values, so they form the range.
~{-3, 8, -1, 4, 1, 0}#This combines input and output values.
= {-3, -1, 1}#Correct. The x-values are -3, -1, and 1.
~{0, 1, 4, 8}#This mixes an input with output values.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q08::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q08] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A function gives the cost \(C(n)\) for buying \(n\) notebooks. Which domain is reasonable?
{
~All real numbers#Notebook counts cannot be negative or fractional in this purchase context.
~All integers#Negative integers are not reasonable counts of notebooks.
~Only positive decimals#A person cannot buy a decimal part of a notebook in this count model.
=Whole numbers such as 0, 1, 2, 3, ...#Correct. The input is a nonnegative whole-number count.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q09::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q09] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
What is the range of the relation \((5,1)\), \((6,1)\), \((7,2)\)?
{
={1, 2}#Correct. Repeated output values are listed once in the range.
~{5, 6, 7}#These are x-values, so they form the domain.
~{1, 1, 2}#Sets do not repeat identical values.
~{5, 6, 7, 1, 2}#This combines domain and range values.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q10::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q10] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A table shows inputs \(1, 2, 3, 4\) and outputs \(12, 10, 8, 6\). Which statement is correct?
{
~The domain is {12, 10, 8, 6}.#Those values are outputs, so they form the range.
=The domain is {1, 2, 3, 4}.#Correct. The domain is the set of input values.
~The range is {1, 2, 3, 4}.#Those values are inputs, so they form the domain.
~The domain and range are the same set.#The input and output sets are different.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q11::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q11] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
Which range is reasonable for the height of water in a tank that can be filled from 0 inches to 48 inches?
{
~All real numbers#The height cannot be negative or exceed 48 inches in this context.
~Only 48 inches#The tank can be partially filled.
=0 to 48 inches, inclusive#Correct. The height can be any amount from empty to full.
~48 inches or more#Values greater than 48 inches exceed the tank height.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q12::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q12] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
What is the domain of the relation \((0,-2)\), \((3,-2)\), and \((6,-2)\)?
{
~{-2}#This is the only output value, so it is the range.
~{0, 3, 6, -2}#This combines domain and range values.
~{-2, 0}#This leaves out two input values.
={0, 3, 6}#Correct. The domain is the set of x-values.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q13::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q13] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
What is the range of the relation \((0,-2)\), \((3,-2)\), and \((6,-2)\)?
{
= {-2}#Correct. The only output value is -2, and repeated values are listed once.
~{0, 3, 6}#These are input values, so they form the domain.
~{0, -2, 3, 6}#This combines inputs and outputs.
~{-2, -2, -2}#A set lists repeated values only once.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q14::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q14] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A vending machine function uses \(d\) for the number of dollars inserted. If the machine accepts only whole dollar bills from 0 to 10, which domain is reasonable?
{
~All real numbers from 0 to 10#The machine accepts only whole dollar bills, so decimals are not reasonable inputs.
~All integers#Negative integers and values above 10 are outside the context.
~Only 10#The machine may accept amounts less than 10.
={0, 1, 2, 3, ..., 10}#Correct. The input values are whole-dollar amounts from 0 through 10.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q15::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q15] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
Which statement best describes the domain of a function?
{
~The set of possible outputs#That describes the range, not the domain.
=The set of possible inputs#Correct. The domain is the set of input values for a function.
~The largest output value only#A domain is a set of inputs, not a single output.
~The rule used to calculate outputs#The rule defines the function, while the domain describes allowed inputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q16::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q16] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
Which statement best describes the range of a function?
{
~The set of possible inputs#That describes the domain.
~The x-values only#Range is based on output values, which are usually y-values.
=The set of possible outputs#Correct. The range is the set of output values.
~The independent variable#The independent variable is connected to inputs, not outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q17::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q17] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
The points \((1,10)\), \((2,20)\), \((3,30)\), and \((4,40)\) are shown on a graph. Which set is the range?
{
={10, 20, 30, 40}#Correct. The range is the set of y-values.
~{1, 2, 3, 4}#These are x-values, so they form the domain.
~{1, 10, 2, 20, 3, 30, 4, 40}#This combines x-values and y-values.
~{10, 20, 30}#This leaves out one output value.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q18::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q18] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A function models the cost of buying movie tickets. Which output unit should appear in the range?
{
~tickets#Tickets are the input count in this context.
=dollars#Correct. The output of a cost function is measured in dollars.
~hours#Time is not the output unit in this ticket cost context.
~people#The function models cost, not a number of people.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q19::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q19] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
For a function that gives temperature over a 24-hour day, which domain is reasonable if \(t\) is measured in hours after midnight?
{
~All real numbers#The context limits time to one 24-hour day.
~Only whole numbers from 0 to 24#This misses possible times such as 2.5 hours after midnight.
=0 to 24 hours, inclusive#Correct. Time can vary continuously from the start to the end of the day.
~Temperatures from 0 to 24 degrees#Those are output-type values, not time inputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q20::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q20] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
What is the domain of the relation shown by \(x\)-values 4, 8, 12 and \(y\)-values 1, 3, 5?
{
~{1, 3, 5}#These are y-values, so they form the range.
~{4, 1, 8, 3, 12, 5}#This combines input and output values.
~{1, 4, 5, 12}#This mixes inputs and outputs and omits values.
={4, 8, 12}#Correct. The domain is the set of x-values.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q21::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q21] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A graph starts at \((0,2)\) and ends at \((6,14)\), including every point between them. What domain is shown?
{
=0 to 6, inclusive#Correct. The x-values begin at 0, end at 6, and include all values between.
~2 to 14, inclusive#Those are y-values, so they describe the range.
~Only {0, 6}#The graph includes every point between the endpoints, not just the endpoints.
~All real numbers#The graph is limited to x-values from 0 through 6.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q22::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q22] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A graph starts at \((0,2)\) and ends at \((6,14)\), including every point between them. What range is shown?
{
~0 to 6, inclusive#Those are x-values, so they describe the domain.
=2 to 14, inclusive#Correct. The y-values begin at 2, end at 14, and include all values between.
~Only {2, 14}#The graph includes every point between the endpoints, so the output values are continuous.
~All real numbers#The graph is limited to y-values from 2 through 14.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q23::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q23] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
Which situation is most likely to have a domain of whole numbers only?
{
~The temperature outside during a day#Time and temperature can vary continuously.
~The height of water in a tank#Height can include decimal measurements.
=The number of books bought at a store#Correct. Book counts are nonnegative whole numbers.
~The distance traveled by a car#Distance can include fractional values.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q24::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q24] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
Which situation is most likely to have a continuous range?
{
~The number of students in a class#Student counts are whole numbers, not continuous values.
~The number of notebooks purchased#Notebook counts are discrete whole numbers.
~The number of text messages sent#Message counts are discrete whole numbers.
=The height of a plant over time#Correct. Height can take decimal values and changes continuously.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_Q25::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_Q25] [MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support]
A table lists \(x\): 2, 4, 6 and \(f(x)\): 11, 15, 19. Which statement is correct?
{
=The domain is {2, 4, 6} and the range is {11, 15, 19}.#Correct. Inputs form the domain, and outputs form the range.
~The domain is {11, 15, 19} and the range is {2, 4, 6}.#This reverses the input and output sets.
~The domain and range are both {2, 4, 6, 11, 15, 19}.#This combines all values instead of separating inputs from outputs.
~The range is {2, 4, 6}.#Those are input values, not output values.
}
